# Deployment and first-run contract

Use `D:\Cursor Project\POSTILKA NEW` as a read-only reference for deployment patterns only. Adapt its routing and Compose ideas to this simulator. Never modify the reference project or copy its secrets, domain, credentials, product features, or production-specific assumptions.

## Current deployment target

- Canonical domain: `burilka.com`.
- Current server IPv4: `95.163.244.138`.
- Use `https://burilka.com` as the production origin for the public WordPress site (`/`) and application (`/app`).
- Use these values as configuration/documentation defaults only. Before issuing TLS or deploying, verify authoritative DNS A resolution and host reachability. Do not request a `www.burilka.com` certificate or configure an AAAA record unless the owner confirms those DNS names/records are configured.
- Keep domain/IP configurable through sanitized environment/configuration where appropriate; never reuse `postilka.ru` or its server IP in this project.

## Docker service boundaries

The root Compose topology keeps independently deployable/runtime services in separate containers:

- `nginx`: only public ingress; ports 80/443; TLS termination, ACME challenge routing, reverse proxy, security headers, upload/body/time limits.
- `frontend`: Next.js standalone server, with `basePath: '/app'`; no database credentials.
- `backend`: Go HTTP API; connects to PostgreSQL and only required internal services.
- `worker`: same backend image is permitted, but starts a distinct worker command; no public host port. Keep API and worker resource/concurrency limits independent.
- `postgres`: application source-of-truth database; private Compose network only, persistent named volume, never publish its port publicly.
- `wordpress`: separate WordPress/PHP web container for marketing pages at `/`; persistent uploads and configuration as needed.
- `mysql`: dedicated WordPress database only; private Compose network and persistent named volume; never store simulator application data here.
- Optional `certbot`/ACME helper is a separate one-shot or scheduled container only if its lifecycle is explicit. It must share only the ACME webroot and certificate volumes it needs.

Do not add NATS, Redis/Dragonfly, object storage, or other services until a concrete requirement and operational plan justify them. If 3D assets use an external object store/CDN, document the endpoint, CORS/cache policy, backup/retention, and local development approach.

Use health checks and `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` for required database/bootstrap dependencies. `depends_on` alone is not proof that a service is ready. Keep internal databases off host ports by default. Use named volumes for database state, restart policies, bounded JSON logs, init/graceful shutdown, and resource limits in production.

## Routing contract

All public services use one configured domain and path-based routing:

| Public path | Destination |
| --- | --- |
| `/` and ordinary marketing URLs | WordPress |
| `/wp-admin`, `/wp-content`, `/wp-includes`, `/wp-json`, `/wp-login.php`, setup/install paths | WordPress |
| `/app` and `/app/...` including Next assets | Next.js frontend |
| `/app/api/v1/...` | Go API; Nginx may strip `/app` only if the API and SSR internal URL contract explicitly require it |
| `/app/health` | Go API health endpoint |
| `/.well-known/acme-challenge/...` on HTTP | ACME webroot; must remain reachable during issuance and renewal |

- Frontend routes include `/app/auth/login`, `/app/auth/register`, and `/app/admin/`. The admin UI route is not itself a security boundary: every admin API and server action must verify a platform-superadmin permission.
- Use a single canonical API prefix and document the exact prefix-stripping behavior in Nginx, Next.js, browser API client, and backend routes. Do not create ambiguous double-prefix paths.
- Preserve forwarded host/proto/client headers. Set appropriate proxy timeouts and body limits per route; disable inappropriate caching for authenticated HTML/API responses. Cache only fingerprinted static assets and immutable public scene assets.
- WordPress must not claim or shadow `/app`, app APIs, or Next static assets. Test routing with trailing slashes and encoded/path-normalized inputs.

## Makefile is the operator interface

Create a root `Makefile`; running plain `make` must display concise help and must not perform state-changing work. Keep targets phony and fail fast. At minimum expose:

- `make help`: list targets and indicate dev vs production behavior.
- `make setup`: check required tools, create local `.env` from a sanitized example only if absent, create required local directories, and explain next steps; never overwrite an existing `.env`.
- `make up` / `make down`: start/stop local Compose services; no production overlay.
- `make build`: build local images without silently deploying.
- `make prod` (or clearly named `deploy`): production Compose build/start followed by migrations in a reviewed order and public-edge smoke checks. Never treat local `make up` as production.
- `make ps`, `make logs [SERVICE=...]`, `make test`, `make lint`, `make migrate` (explicit, safe direction only), `make wp-install`/`wp-bootstrap`, `make superadmin-bootstrap`, `make ssl-init`, `make ssl-renew`, and `make verify-release`.
- Production-specific commands require an explicit prod Compose selection and existing validated production env. Destructive reset, volume deletion, database restore, and secret rotation must be separate conspicuously named operations with confirmation; do not hide them behind `down` or `prod`.
- Use `docker compose` (Compose v2 plugin), not the obsolete `docker-compose` v1 command. Make recipes must use tabs, quote arguments safely, avoid echoing environment secrets, and propagate command failures.
- Add `make help` as the default target. Keep the normal deploy path reproducible from documented targets rather than shell history or editor tasks.

## Host prerequisites and first production deployment

Document an Ubuntu LTS first-deploy guide under `docs/deploy/` (or `scripts/`) covering these ordered steps. Commands must be reviewed for the chosen Ubuntu version and official Docker repository; do not assume a package name exists on every release.

1. Provision a supported Ubuntu LTS host and DNS A/AAAA records for the chosen domain. Ensure DNS resolves to this host; if HTTP-01 is used, inbound TCP 80 must be reachable from the public Internet. Do not publish database ports.
2. Install Git, Make, Docker Engine, and the official Docker Compose v2 plugin; enable/start Docker. Verify `docker --version` and `docker compose version`. Explain non-root Docker group access as root-equivalent and require re-login after group membership changes.
3. Configure host firewall to allow SSH, HTTP 80, and HTTPS 443 only as required. Confirm the provider/network firewall also permits 80/443.
4. Clone the repository into a documented deployment directory. Copy `.env.production.example` to an untracked `.env` only if absent; generate unique strong secrets; set domain, database credentials, WordPress bootstrap fields, and superadmin bootstrap secret via protected input. Never put actual credentials in docs or shell command history.
5. Validate the production env without printing values. Do not start the final HTTPS Nginx configuration before the certificate files exist unless it has a deliberate HTTP-only bootstrap configuration.
6. Obtain a free Let's Encrypt certificate. Preferred design: start an HTTP-only Nginx service that serves `/.well-known/acme-challenge/` from a persistent ACME webroot, then run Certbot `certonly --webroot` for the configured apex and optional `www` names. Alternatively use standalone Certbot only while Nginx is stopped and port 80 is released; document the interruption and restart order. Do not assume `www` is configured—request only names with correct DNS.
7. Persist `/etc/letsencrypt` and the challenge webroot in protected volumes/bind mounts. Validate certificate SANs/expiry and file readability by Nginx without copying private keys into the source tree or image. Lock down host-side private-key permissions.
8. Start the production stack with Nginx HTTPS enabled. Verify certificate chain, SNI/domain, HTTP→HTTPS redirect, ACME challenge access, route table, health endpoints, DB readiness, and logs with secrets redacted.
9. Run ordered database migrations once as part of the release procedure. Ensure migration execution is safe when API replicas start; never use automatic destructive schema resets.
10. Complete WordPress first-run installation and superadmin provisioning as described below; verify `/` displays the installed marketing site and `/app` displays the application.
11. Run `certbot renew --dry-run` (or equivalent for the chosen ACME client). Configure a host timer or dedicated renewal job that renews before expiry and signals/reloads Nginx only after successful renewal. Monitoring/alerting must cover expiry and failed renewal.
12. Run `make verify-release` and record the release/deployment report. Do not claim production readiness if any routing, TLS, data persistence, role/bootstrap, health, or renewal check fails.

TLS and ACME rules:

- Let's Encrypt is free but certificates expire and must renew automatically; obtaining one once is not sufficient.
- Keep HTTP port 80 available for HTTP-01 redirects/challenges. Serve ACME challenge paths before the general HTTPS redirect.
- Never commit `fullchain.pem`, `privkey.pem`, ACME account keys, or certificates into Git or Docker images. Do not expose private keys to frontend/backend/WordPress containers.
- Renewal must be idempotent, logged without secrets, alert on failure, and trigger a safe Nginx reload after certificate replacement.
- Document staging/dry-run validation to avoid production CA rate limits during development.

## WordPress first-run bootstrap

- On the first production `make prod`, WordPress and its dedicated MySQL service start with persistent volumes; database health is checked before installation.
- Provide an idempotent `wp-bootstrap`/one-shot bootstrap job or documented `make wp-install` that installs WordPress if and only if the database is not already installed, then sets the site URL/title, HTTPS-aware proxy settings, permalinks, locale, and initial admin account from protected environment input.
- Do not run an installation routine on every container restart if WordPress is already installed. Existing-site updates must not reset admin credentials or overwrite content.
- The bootstrap waits for MySQL and WordPress readiness, returns nonzero on partial failure, and can safely be rerun. Never use sample passwords or echo credentials in output.
- Keep the public marketing site at `/`; configure reverse-proxy HTTPS awareness so WordPress does not loop between HTTP and HTTPS. Restrict or harden XML-RPC and setup paths as appropriate without blocking required WordPress functions.
- Persist WordPress database and uploads independently and document backup/restore. The public marketing CMS is not the application admin or student data store.

## Initial platform superadmin bootstrap

- The product owner supplies the superadmin email (initial requested account: `erman.ai@yandex.ru`). Do not hardcode an initial password in this repository or these rules.
- Provide a one-time `make superadmin-bootstrap` flow backed by a backend provisioning command. Read password from a secure interactive prompt or protected secret input (not a command-line argument, checked-in file, Compose literal, or log). Never print it.
- Create or promote the account idempotently. If it already exists, do not overwrite its password; require a separate authenticated password-reset flow. Grant only the platform-superadmin role, audit the bootstrap event, and require password change on first login.
- The route `/app/admin/` is available only to authenticated platform superadmins. Enforce authorization on every admin API operation server-side. Public registration must never permit a client to select or self-assign this role.
- Treat any password previously shared in chat or docs as compromised: rotate it and use a new unique secret before the first production bootstrap.

## Production update and recovery

- Separate build, migration, and service rollout steps. Rebuild/recreate only changed services where safe; run edge health checks after each release.
- Back up PostgreSQL, WordPress MySQL, WordPress uploads, and any external scene/content storage before risky changes; test restore procedures.
- Pin production image tags, use least-privilege networks/volumes, resource and log limits, health checks, graceful shutdown, and restart policies.
- Keep databases and internal worker services on private Compose networks. Only Nginx publishes ports to the host.
- Deployment instructions must state rollback limitations for schema and content migrations; do not promise rollback by simply reverting an image if a migration is irreversible.
- Never commit/push/deploy or mutate production data unless the user explicitly requests the operation.